

**16. Jacobian Elliptic Functions and Theta Functions**

**Mathematical Properties**

**Jacobian Elliptic Functions**

**16.1. Introduction**

**A doubly periodic meromorphic function is called an** _elliptic function_**.**

**Let** _m,_ **m**_1 be numbers such that**

\[\begin{array}{c}m{+}m_{1}{=}1.\end{array}\]

**We call** \(m\) **the** _parameter,_ **m**_1 **the** _complementary parameter_**.**

**In what follows we shall assume that the parameter** \(m\) **is a real number. Without loss of generality we can then suppose that** \(0{\leq}m{\leq}1\) **(see** 16.10, 16.11**).**

**We define** _quarter-periods_****\(K\) **and** \(iK^{\prime}\) **by**

\[\begin{array}{c}K(m){=}K{=}\int_{0}^{\sigma/2}\frac{d\theta}{(1{-}m\,\sin^{2}{\theta})^{1/2}},\\ \qquad\qquad iK^{\prime}(m){=}iK^{\prime}{=}i{\int_{0}^{\sigma/2}}\frac{d\theta}{(1{-}m_{1}\sin^{2}{\theta})^{1/2}}\end{array}\]

**so that** \(K\) **and** \(K^{\prime}\) **are real numbers.** \(K\) **is called the real,** \(iK^{\prime}\) **the imaginary quarter-period.**

**We note that**

\[\begin{array}{c}K(m){=}K^{\prime}(m_{1}){=}K^{\prime}(1{-}m).\end{array}\]

**We also note that if any** _one_ **of the numbers** _m,_ **m**_1,_ **K(_m_1),_ **K'(_m_2),_ **K'(_m_2)/_K(_m_3) is given, all the rest are determined. Thus** \(K\) **and** \(K^{\prime}\) **can not both be chosen arbitrarily.**

**In the Argand diagram denote the points** \(0,\,K,\)****\(K{+}iK^{\prime}\)**,** \(iK^{\prime}\) **by s, c, d, n respectively. These points are at the vertices of a rectangle. The translations of this rectangle by** \(\lambda K\)**,** \(\mu{iK^{\prime}}\)**, where** \(\lambda\)**,** \(\mu\) **are given all integral values positive or negative, will lead to the lattice**

\[\begin{array}{c}.s\quad.c\quad.s\quad.c\\ .n\quad.d\quad.n\quad.d\quad\\ .s\quad.c\quad.s\quad.c\quad.n\quad.d\quad\\ .n\quad.d\quad.n\quad.d\quad\end{array}\]

**the pattern being repeated indefinitely on all sides.**

**Let** **p, q be any two of the letters s, c, d, n. Then p, q determine in the lattice a minimum rectangle whose sides are of length** \(K\) **and** \(K^{\prime}\) **and whose vertices s, c, d, n are in counterclockwise order.**

**Definition**

**The Jacobian elliptic function pq** \(u\) **is defined by the following three properties.**

**(i) pq** \(u\) **has a simple zero at p and a simple pole at q.**

**(ii) The step from p to q is a half period of pq** \(u\)**. Those of the numbers** \(K,iK^{\prime},K{+}iK^{\prime}\) **which differ from this step are only quarter-periods.**

**(iii) The coefficient of the leading term in the expansion of pq** \(u\) **in ascending powers of** \(u\) **about** \(u{=}0\) **is unity. With regard to (iii) the leading term is** \(u\)**,** \(1/u\)**,** \(1\) **according as** \(u{=}0\) **is a zero, a pole, or an ordinary point.**

**Thus the functions with a pole or zero at the origin (i.e., the functions in which one letter is s) are odd, and the others are even.**

**Should we wish to call explicit attention to the value of the parameter, we write pq** \((u|m)\) **instead of pq** \(u\)**.**

